(window.GROUP_QUIZZES=window.GROUP_QUIZZES||{})["pharmacology-i-exam-2-pharm-e2-mi-vignettes-version-2"]={"title":"Myocardial Ischemia Drugs — Clinical Vignettes 2","category":"Pharmacology I","exam":"Exam 2","sem":"fall-2026","questions":[{"q":"A 64-year-old man with stable angina takes verapamil. His clinician adds simvastatin for hyperlipidemia, and three weeks later he reports diffuse muscle pain and weakness. Which interaction explains his symptoms?","choices":["Lowered simvastatin levels","Raised simvastatin levels","Additive atrioventricular block","Faster verapamil clearance"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Verapamil inhibits CYP3A4 (cytochrome P450 3A4), the enzyme that clears simvastatin, so statin levels rise and the risk of muscle toxicity increases. — Antihypertensives.pptx, Slide 47"},{"q":"A 58-year-old man has had squeezing chest pressure on climbing stairs for three months, relieved within minutes by rest. He had a myocardial infarction two years ago. Heart rate is 76 beats per minute and blood pressure is 132/80 mmHg. He has no asthma and no conduction disease. Which drug class is first-line daily therapy to prevent further attacks?","choices":["A long-acting nitrate","A dihydropyridine calcium channel blocker","An angiotensin-converting enzyme inhibitor","A beta blocker"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Beta blockers are first-line for chronic stable angina when nothing forbids them, and they are favored after a myocardial infarction. They lower heart rate and contractility, which cuts oxygen demand. — Myocardial Ischemia Drugs.pptx, Slide 17"},{"q":"A 57-year-old man with hypertension and stable angina has a blood pressure of 154/94 mmHg and a heart rate of 82 beats per minute. His clinician plans a calcium channel blocker. Which calcium channel blocker should be avoided?","choices":["Short-acting nifedipine","Long-acting nifedipine","Amlodipine","Felodipine"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Its abrupt vasodilation causes hypotension and reflex tachycardia, which can worsen ischemia, so short-acting agents are avoided in angina. — Myocardial Ischemia Drugs.pptx, Slide 22"},{"q":"A 49-year-old man takes metoprolol daily for exertional angina. He has felt well for weeks and plans to stop the tablets. Which counseling point is most important?","choices":["Keep the tablets in the original bottle","Never stop it suddenly","Eat extra fiber to prevent constipation","Place one tablet under the tongue"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Rapid discontinuation of a beta blocker is to be avoided, so it is stopped only on the advice of the prescriber rather than on the patient's own. — Myocardial Ischemia Drugs.pptx, Slide 19"},{"q":"A 62-year-old man has exertional angina and severe asthma needing daily inhaled bronchodilators. Heart rate is 78 beats per minute, blood pressure is 130/80 mmHg, ventricular function is normal, and there is no conduction disease. Which is the most appropriate initial antianginal?","choices":["Diltiazem","Propranolol","Isosorbide mononitrate","Enalapril"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. A non-dihydropyridine calcium channel blocker is the initial therapy when a beta blocker is contraindicated or not tolerated, and it does not provoke bronchospasm. — Myocardial Ischemia Drugs.pptx, Slide 22"},{"q":"A 60-year-old woman receives alteplase for an ST-elevation myocardial infarction. Two hours later she has a sudden severe headache, vomiting, confusion and right-sided weakness. Which adverse effect is most likely?","choices":["Ventricular arrhythmia","Intracranial hemorrhage","Anaphylactic reaction","Allergic skin rash"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Bleeding is the main risk of fibrinolytics, and this sudden headache with a focal deficit points to intracranial hemorrhage. — Myocardial Ischemia Drugs.pptx, Slide 65"},{"q":"A 58-year-old man has substernal pressure whenever he climbs two flights of stairs, relieved by a few minutes of rest. This pattern has been unchanged in frequency and severity for two years, with no rest pain. The resting electrocardiogram is normal and cardiac biomarkers are normal. What is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":["Unstable angina","Variant angina","Chronic stable angina","Non-ST-elevation myocardial infarction"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Exertional pressure that is relieved by rest, has been stable for two years and comes with normal biomarkers is chronic stable angina, graded by the activity that provokes it. — Myocardial Ischemia Drugs.pptx, Slide 15"},{"q":"A 59-year-old woman with type 2 diabetes mellitus has exertional angina. She has no history of myocardial infarction, heart failure or hypertension, and no asthma. Heart rate is 76 beats per minute and blood pressure is 124/76 mmHg. In the traditional comorbidity-based approach, which class is first line?","choices":["A non-cardioselective beta blocker","A dihydropyridine calcium channel blocker","A short-acting nitrate","A non-dihydropyridine calcium channel blocker"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. With diabetes and no other reason for a beta blocker, the traditional table gives a non-dihydropyridine as first line; a long-acting nitrate or a cardioselective beta blocker is the alternative. Current practice is more flexible and still accepts a cardioselective beta blocker first. — Myocardial Ischemia Drugs.pptx, Slide 34"},{"q":"A 63-year-old woman has exertional angina three times a week despite maximally tolerated metoprolol plus amlodipine. Blood pressure is 122/74 mmHg and heart rate is 62 beats per minute. What is the most appropriate next step?","choices":["Adding verapamil","Coronary angiography","Switching to short-acting nifedipine","Stopping both for a nitrate alone"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Persistent angina on two antianginals prompts further workup, such as angiography, before more drugs are stacked on top. — Myocardial Ischemia Drugs.pptx, Slide 36"},{"q":"A 61-year-old man with stable angina develops chest pressure while carrying groceries. Heart rate is 80 beats per minute and blood pressure is 136/84 mmHg. He takes metoprolol, amlodipine and isosorbide mononitrate every day. Which drug should he use to stop this attack?","choices":["Metoprolol","Amlodipine","Isosorbide mononitrate","Nitroglycerin spray"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Short-acting nitroglycerin under the tongue is the drug for quick relief of an attack; the daily drugs are for prevention. — Myocardial Ischemia Drugs.pptx, Slide 27"},{"q":"A 58-year-old man with a non-ST-elevation myocardial infarction is taken for percutaneous coronary intervention with a stent. Which drug class is used before the procedure and to prevent clots on the stent?","choices":["A glycoprotein IIb/IIIa inhibitor","A fibrinolytic","A P2Y12 receptor antagonist","A nitrate"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. These antiplatelet drugs are used before percutaneous coronary intervention and as clot prophylaxis after stent placement. — Myocardial Ischemia Drugs.pptx, Slide 66"},{"q":"A 36-year-old woman with no cardiac risk factors wakes at 4 a.m. with severe chest pressure at rest. The electrocardiogram shows transient ST-segment elevation that resolves after sublingual nitroglycerin, and cardiac biomarkers are normal. What is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":["Variant (Prinzmetal) angina","ST-elevation myocardial infarction","Chronic stable angina","Unstable angina"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Vasospastic angina occurs in younger patients with few risk factors, often at night or in the early morning, with transient ST-segment elevation. — Myocardial Ischemia Drugs.pptx, Slide 8"},{"q":"A 63-year-old man with an acute coronary syndrome has a heart rate of 54 beats per minute, blood pressure of 118/72 mmHg and second-degree heart block on the electrocardiogram. Which drug should be withheld?","choices":["Aspirin","Metoprolol","Sublingual nitroglycerin","Enoxaparin"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. A beta blocker needs caution with bradycardia and heart block, and this patient has a slow heart rate and second-degree block. — Myocardial Ischemia Drugs.pptx, Slide 55"},{"q":"A 63-year-old man has three hours of chest pressure. The electrocardiogram shows ST-segment depression, and the cardiac biomarker is elevated. He has no bleeding history. Which therapy is not recommended for him?","choices":["Aspirin","Metoprolol","Reteplase","Enoxaparin"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Fibrinolytics are not recommended in non-ST-elevation acute coronary syndrome, because the bleeding risk outweighs the benefit. — Myocardial Ischemia Drugs.pptx, Slide 68"},{"q":"A 59-year-old man with exertional angina is given nitroglycerin tablets for attacks. How should he be told to take one?","choices":["Place it under the tongue","Swallow it whole with water","Chew it, then swallow","Crush it into his food"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Short-acting nitrates are applied or sprayed under the tongue, which gives rapid onset for an acute attack. — Myocardial Ischemia Drugs.pptx, Slide 28"},{"q":"A 74-year-old man has exertional chest pressure and two episodes of syncope. A crescendo-decrescendo systolic murmur radiates to the carotids, and echocardiography confirms severe aortic stenosis. Which drug is contraindicated for his chest pain?","choices":["Aspirin","Clopidogrel","Metoprolol","Nitroglycerin"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Aortic valve stenosis is a listed nitrate contraindication, because the vasodilation they cause is poorly tolerated with a fixed outflow obstruction. — Myocardial Ischemia Drugs.pptx, Slide 33"},{"q":"A 64-year-old man with known angina develops chest pressure at rest. He sits down and places one nitroglycerin tablet under his tongue. Five minutes later the pain is unchanged. What should he do next?","choices":["Wait an hour to see if rest helps","Swallow a long-acting nitrate tablet","Take his beta blocker early","Call emergency services"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Chest pain unrelieved five minutes after the first nitroglycerin dose is treated as a possible acute coronary syndrome, so emergency help is called. — Myocardial Ischemia Drugs.pptx, Slide 27"},{"q":"A 69-year-old man has exertional angina, a heart rate of 46 beats per minute and second-degree atrioventricular block on the electrocardiogram. Which antianginal is most appropriate?","choices":["Atenolol","Verapamil","Diltiazem","Amlodipine"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. With bradycardia or atrioventricular block, a dihydropyridine is first-line, because it acts on vessels and does not slow conduction. — Myocardial Ischemia Drugs.pptx, Slide 34"},{"q":"A 58-year-old man has had crushing chest pressure with sweating for 30 minutes, and the ambulance crew records ST-segment elevation. He has no allergy and no bleeding history. Which drug reduces mortality and reinfarction and is given first?","choices":["Sublingual nitroglycerin","Oral metoprolol","Chewed aspirin","Intravenous morphine"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Aspirin is given at the first signs of chest pain, chewed and swallowed, and it reduces mortality and reinfarction. — Myocardial Ischemia Drugs.pptx, Slide 52"},{"q":"A 61-year-old man develops fever, chills and a skin rash shortly after a fibrinolytic infusion for a myocardial infarction. There is no bleeding and blood pressure is stable. Which agent was most likely given?","choices":["Streptokinase","Alteplase","Tenecteplase","Heparin"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Fever, chills and rash are allergic reactions mainly seen with streptokinase, the bacterial protein. — Myocardial Ischemia Drugs.pptx, Slide 62"},{"q":"A 44-year-old woman with well-controlled asthma, treated with a daily inhaled corticosteroid and an albuterol rescue inhaler, has exertional angina. She has no bradycardia or conduction disease. Which beta blocker is most appropriate?","choices":["Metoprolol","Propranolol","Nadolol","Labetalol"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. It is beta-1 selective, so it has less effect on the airway beta-2 receptors that matter in asthma. Non-selective agents are the ones to avoid in reactive airway disease. — Myocardial Ischemia Drugs.pptx, Slide 34"},{"q":"A 66-year-old man has chest pressure at rest and an electrocardiogram suggesting an acute coronary syndrome. He had an intracranial hemorrhage last week. Blood pressure is 130/80 mmHg and heart rate is 80 beats per minute. Which of the following is contraindicated?","choices":["Nitroglycerin","Metoprolol","Aspirin","Morphine"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Recent intracranial hemorrhage is a listed contraindication to aspirin, along with allergy and recent gastrointestinal bleeding. — Myocardial Ischemia Drugs.pptx, Slide 53"},{"q":"A 61-year-old man with ST-elevation myocardial infarction has received chewed aspirin and sublingual nitroglycerin. Heart rate is 88 beats per minute, blood pressure is 134/82 mmHg, the lungs are clear, and there is no heart block or asthma. In the traditional approach, which added class is given to lower early mortality?","choices":["A non-dihydropyridine calcium channel blocker","An opioid analgesic","A beta blocker","A long-acting nitrate"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. A beta blocker is traditionally added, intravenous first and then oral, and is credited with lower early and late mortality when heart rate and pressure allow. Current guidelines favor oral therapy in the first day. — Myocardial Ischemia Drugs.pptx, Slide 55"},{"q":"A 34-year-old woman is diagnosed with variant angina after recurrent nighttime rest pain with transient ST-segment elevation. Which class should be avoided?","choices":["Calcium channel blockers","Beta blockers","Long-acting nitrates","Sublingual nitroglycerin"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. In variant angina beta blockers may worsen symptoms, so calcium channel blockers and nitrates are used instead. — Myocardial Ischemia Drugs.pptx, Slide 39"}]};
